  "review_statement": "## Problem and change\n\nReceive the qualified Commons, Eclipse, Guava-fork, and Spring collection improvements into the current M3JDK owners. The 16-file composed result preserves existing target packages, licences, and newer capabilities, including the array-list two-long mask path for <=128 elements, value epochs, and bulk-add behavior. Jackson's adapter change is prepared in its fork; this target has no corresponding owner.\n\nA separate qualified one-file test correction repairs stale cursor-fault reachability: the prior true predicate returned a visible nearest element before opening the armed cursor. The corrected fixture reaches the fault, retains exception identity, explicitly checks cursor entry, and separately protects the fast path. Production filtered-view bytes remain unchanged.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Actual current collection module: **772 tests; 770 passed, zero failures/errors, two optional complete-native-lane tests skipped**, using arrays/mask JNI providers under -Xcheck:jni.\n- Focused Commons receiving suite on the final notice-complete source: **376 passed**. Eclipse: **254 passed** on pre-format source, followed by **9 final-format regression tests**. Spring receiver: **17 tests in both real Java 21 VM modes** on the final notice-complete owner.\n- M3ArrayList all declared compiled descriptors match baseline.\n- Corrected native harness: **23 JVM tests with no skips** plus explicit native-required window/compactor tests and separate original C programs.\n- The fixture preserves its **1,008-case successful-navigation digest**, passed **11 focused tests**, and received an independent exact-diff review.\n- All seven source datasets passed actual SDK replay, source sealing, receiver refusal cases, and zero-change fixed-point receive.\n\nThe final notice correction changed only Commons/Spring implementation comments. Their module checks were repeated; the main-module owner and test bytes remain identical to the 772-test run.\n\n## Open merge gates\n\nThe existing naming gate refuses the Arrays.java companion source hash on both exact baseline and candidate. Commons/Eclipse inherited style and licence-header-rule mismatches remain recorded. Full installed-JDK/HotSpot/platform/jtreg qualification and the complete optional native lane are not claimed. No source hash was resealed merely to bypass a gate. This PR stays draft for those promotion gates and does not claim whole-fleet conversion or measured speedup.\n\n## Canonical execution\n\nCanonical recipe: `com.synexia.m3jdk.CollectionsContractRefinement20261017`, source revision **7af83c5ee4ad166d8cd45c6c33822debabd705ca**, review https://github.com/hsoliwal/com.synexia/pull/15427. Product bytes were emitted by actual qualified SDK Results and the second receive changed zero files. The canonical recipe and executable receiver remain in Synexia.\n",
